<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Sitrep.Host/Sitrep.Host.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../Sitrep.Contract.TestSupport/Sitrep.Contract.TestSupport.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- Stock CommNet's OCCLUSION declaration, a deliberately KSP-free file
       carved out of Gonogo.KSP, so CommsOcclusionTests asserts against the REAL
       declaration rather than re-stated constants. A ProjectReference is not an
       option, since that assembly needs a live KSP. Only core source belongs
       here: an Uplink's declaration is pinned in that Uplink's own Tests
       project, which leaves with it, and the election tests compete stock
       against a planted model instead. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="../Gonogo.KSP/CommNetOcclusion.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- NO Reinforced.Typings reference. There was one, added so this test host
       could resolve the [TsEnum]/[TsInterface] attributes the shipped contract
       used to carry: Enum.TryParse<TargetKind> walks an enum's attributes
       looking for FlagsAttribute, so it threw FileNotFoundException on a type
       nothing in the test even mentioned.

       Supplying the missing DLL treated the symptom and re-armed the trap. A
       resolvable copy beside a test binary is all a behavioural test needs to
       go green, so the leak became invisible exactly where it should have been
       loudest. Measured: with the leak present and a copy in bin, the contract
       enum tests pass 5 of 6; delete the one file and the same build fails 6
       of 6. That is the 2026-08-20 incident's mechanism.

       The attributes now live only in the *.Contract.Codegen twins, so nothing
       here needs the DLL. Do not add it back to make a test pass; a test that
       needs it is telling you the leak is back. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The CI "lying minor" contract-shape gate's baseline; see
         ContractShapeGateTests's doc comment. Wired in the same
         golden-fixtures/ pattern Sitrep.Core.Tests already uses. -->
    <None Include="../Sitrep.Contract/contract-shape.baseline.json" Link="golden-fixtures/contract-shape.baseline.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
